- How would you handle varying campaign setup costs or penalties per segment length?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP over partitioning with an order-dependent gain, plus reconstruction. Distinct from standard sum-of-maximums or cooldown problems.

## Key Concepts

- Order-dependent block weighting
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- 2D DP with backtracking pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // end a block at i
      for (let j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + (pref[i+1] - pref[j]) * t;
        if (val > dp[i+1][t]) {
          dp[i+1][t] = val;
          par[i+1][t] = j;
        }
      }
      // skip i is implicit via dp[i+1][t] initialization from dp[i][t]
      if (dp[i][t] > dp[i+1][t]) {
        dp[i+1][t] = dp[i][t];
        par[i+1][t] = -2; // indicate skip
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to optimize to near O(nk) for special A (e.g., non-decreasing) or under additional constraints?
- How would you extend to allow at most k blocks or to recover the exact endpoints efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with partitioning and a nontrivial per-substring cost, plus backtracking to recover partitions.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t]
- Precompute cost[l][r] in O(n^2) via two-pointer mismatches
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPalindromePartitions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      let a = l, b = r, c = 0;
      while (a < b) { if (s[a] !== s[b]) c++; a++; b--; }
      cost[l][r] = c;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = cost[0][i];
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + cost[p + 1][i];
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; parent[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  const splits = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t >= 1) {
    splits.push(i);
    i = parent[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  splits.reverse();
  return { minCost: dp[n - 1][k], partitions: splits };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize space for large n or small k?
- How would this adapt if cost used a different per-substring metric?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a beginner-friendly DP pattern with non-adjacent selections and reconstruction. It also shows handling multiple dimensions (i and t) and a simple heuristic constraint (no two boosts on consecutive days).

## Key Concepts
- DP with two states per index for constrained selections
- Reconstruction of chosen days
- Time/space: O(nk); note can optimize to O(k) space with rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxWorkload(workload, k) {
  const n = workload.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const noBoost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const boost = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  noBoost[0][0] = workload[0];
  if (k >= 1) boost[0][1] = 2 * workload[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      noBoost[i][t] = Math.max(noBoost[i-1][t], boost[i-1][t]) + workload[i];
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      if (noBoost[i-1][t-1] > NEG) boost[i][t] = noBoost[i-1][t-1] + 2*workload[i];
    }
  }
  let best = NEG, bestT = 0, bestS = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    if (noBoost[n-1][t] > best) { best = noBoost[n-1][t]; bestT = t; bestS = 0; }
    if (boost[n-1][t] > best) { best = boost[n-1][t]; bestT = t; bestS = 1; }
  }
  // reconstruction would backtrack using saved states (omitted for brevity)
  return { maxValue: best, boostedIndices: [] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP skills with an additional dimension for tracking turns while handling obstacles and grid boundaries. It verifies understanding of initialization logic, edge cases, and how to aggregate the final answer by summing across valid turn counts.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP over cells, turn count, and last direction
- Turn counting via direction changes during transitions
- Correct handling of obstacles and grid boundaries
- Time/space trade-offs and proper base case logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, K) {
  const m = grid.length;
  const n = grid[0].length;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[m-1][n-1] === 1) return 0;

  const dp = Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [0, 0])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = 1; // right
  dp[0][0][0][1] = 1; // down

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        // from left (coming from right)
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t][0]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t-1][1]; // turn
        }
        // from top (coming from down)
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t][1]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t-1][0]; // turn
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let ans = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    ans += dp[m-1][n-1][t][0] + dp[m-1][n-1][t][1];
  }
  return ans;
}
```

This runs in O(m × n × K) time and O(m × n × K) space.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests nuanced DP on partitioning with range-dependent block costs, requiring problem decomposition, state design, and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and block counts
- Efficient range max computation in transitions
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(nums, m) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, m); t++) {
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for (let j = i-1; j >= t-1; j--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, nums[j]);
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + curMax * (i - j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct endpoints
  const ends = [];
  let i = n, t = m;
  while (t > 0) {
    ends.push(i);
    i = par[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][m], ends: ends.reverse()};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests DP partitioning with a non-trivial per-segment cost that depends on both sum and min, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exact k segments
- Precompute minVal(l,r) in O(n^2)
- Transition uses prefix sums and minVal; reconstruct with parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxCost(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  // minVal[l][r]
  const minVal = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));
  for(let l=1;l<=n;l++){
    let m = A[l-1];
    minVal[l][l] = m;
    for(let r=l;r<=n;r++){
      m = (r===l) ? A[l-1] : Math.min(m, A[r-1]);
      minVal[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = (pref[i]-pref[0]) * minVal[1][i]; par[i][1] = 0; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (pref[i]-pref[p]) * minVal[p+1][i];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with a spacing constraint and path reconstruction, a common interview variation.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix with constrained subsequences
- 2D state and reconstruction pointers
- Handling infeasible states and space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// state definitions: dp[i][t], par[i][t]
function solve(A, n, k, d) {
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) dp[i][1] = A[i-1];
  for (let t=2; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
      // skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; par[i][t] = -1; }
      // take i
      if (i - d >= 1 && dp[i-d][t-1] !== NEG) {
        const val = dp[i-d][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = i-d; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {dp, par};
}
```

